Revision history for WWW-Forgejo

{{$NEXT}}

    - Initial release: synchronous Moo client for the Forgejo REST API
      (api/v1).
    - WWW::Forgejo: url/token from constructor options or FORGEJO_URL /
      FORGEJO_TOKEN; url is normalised to the /api/v1 base (base_url).
    - Controllers on the client: misc, users, current_user, repos, orgs,
      teams, notifications, packages, activitypub and admin (users, hooks,
      cron, quota, runners). Paths, parameters and request bodies are checked
      against the official Forgejo swagger spec by the test suite.
    - Per-repository controllers on WWW::Forgejo::Entity::Repo: actions,
      assignees, branches, branch_protections, collaborators, contents, flags,
      forks, git, hooks, issues, keys, labels, milestones, pulls, releases,
      reviewers, stargazers, subscribers, subscription, tags, tag_protections,
      teams, topics and wiki; WWW::Forgejo::API::Repo::Statuses for commit
      statuses. Includes Branches::rename, Flags::check/replace/remove_all,
      Releases::latest/delete_by_tag/get_asset/edit_asset/delete_asset and
      TagProtections::edit. Contents raw, media and get_archive return the
      file bytes unchanged; statuses->combined escapes a slash in the ref.
    - Per-organization controllers on WWW::Forgejo::Entity::Org: actions,
      blocked_users, hooks, labels, members, quota and teams. The
      organization comes from the bound entity, not from an argument.
    - One call style for all controllers: query parameters and request bodies
      are named arguments, no hashref is passed positionally, a collection
      comes back as an arrayref; the parameters that are part of the path
      (owner, repo, index, org, name) stay positional.
    - The controllers cover the operations of the Forgejo swagger spec: the
      repositories, the issues, the pull requests, the actions, the users, the
      organizations, the teams, the activitypub endpoints, the admin quota
      rules and groups and the git diffs. The deprecated alias
      POST /org/{org}/repos is left out, its current form
      POST /orgs/{org}/repos is there.
    - Controllers for the admin quota groups and rules (with the rules and the
      users of a group) and for starring a repository and following a user;
      a quota group and a quota rule are entities.
    - The ActivityPub endpoints are a boundary of the client: actor, inbox and
      outbox need an HTTP signature and answer without one with
      "request signature verification failed"; without federation the paths
      are not registered at all.
    - users->create_token and users->delete_token use the basic authentication
      of the request, so the tokens of another user can be managed;
      packages->upload sends a package to the instance outside /api/v1.
    - Yes/no endpoints (204/404) are answered as booleans via check(); the
      verdict of a response is a method of its own, so a transport that
      fetched the response itself can ask the same question. Release assets
      are uploaded as multipart/form-data.
    - Entity classes (WWW::Forgejo::Entity::*) wrapping the decoded JSON, with
      accessors following the swagger response schemas and the full structure
      on ->data. Client chains such as $forgejo->repos->get(...)->issues hold
      no reference cycle.
    - WWW::Forgejo::Role::HTTP: get/post/put/patch/delete with token auth,
      JSON encoding/decoding, query parameters on every verb, DELETE with a
      body, path segments and query values percent-encoded as UTF-8, each
      part encoded exactly once, and a croak carrying status and message on
      non-2xx and undecodable responses; an empty JSON message or a one-line
      plain-text body (such as "404 page not found") becomes the message of
      the croak, and check croaks on a missing route instead of answering
      "no". Text bodies are decoded by their declared charset; get takes a
      raw option that returns the body bytes unchanged. A single request can
      carry basic authentication instead of the token, a raw body with its
      content type and a base URL outside /api/v1. get follows
      X-Total-Count based auto-pagination (page_size, max_pages) for plain
      arrays and the search envelope, with the default LWP backend as well.
      It also paginates the Actions runs envelope (workflow_runs, total from
      the body's total_count when there is no X-Total-Count header).
    - Pluggable transport via WWW::Forgejo::Role::IO; default backend
      WWW::Forgejo::LWPIO (LWP::UserAgent). Transport-independent
      WWW::Forgejo::HTTPRequest / WWW::Forgejo::HTTPResponse value objects,
      shared with Net::Async::Forgejo. LWPIO hands on the body bytes.
    - LWP::Protocol::https is recommended, not required: https instance URLs
      need it, plain-http instances do not.
    - Offline test suite against a mock transport, with POD coverage and
      spec-conformance checks; live tests (t/90-97) run only with
      TEST_FORGEJO_URL and TEST_FORGEJO_TOKEN set and are verified against
      Forgejo 15.0.7; the docker test instance (make -f Makefile.docker)
      runs 15.0.7.
    - The POD coverage test tells the source form of the POD and the woven
      form of the built distribution apart, checks the documented names in
      both, and checks the kind of a name where the POD states one.
    - A wiki page edited without a title keeps the name it is addressed by:
      the client sends that name as the title.
    - The unused entity classes WWW::Forgejo::Entity::User, ::Email and
      ::CronTask are gone, and the Log::Any import is gone from the modules
      that do not log.
    - The cpanfile holds every prerequisite the code loads: a test reads the
      runtime and the test phase of the cpanfile and wants every module of
      lib/ and of the test suite to be named there, so a lost prerequisite
      fails while the distribution is built and not on the first request.
